   },
   "cite": "fs-id1167793882858",
   "prompt": "Find the directional derivative $D_{\\mathbf{u}}f(x,y)$ of $f(x,y)=3x^{2}y-4xy^{3}+3y^{2}-4x$ in the direction of $\\mathbf{u}=(\\cos \\frac{\\pi}{3})\\mathbf{i}+(\\sin \\frac{\\pi}{3})\\mathbf{j}$ using <a href=\"#fs-id1167794071305\">Equation 14.37</a>. What is $D_{\\mathbf{u}}f(3,4)?$",
   "answer": {
    "type": "open",
    "generated_by": "source",
    "solution": "$\\begin{array}{l} \\\\ \\\\ D_{\\mathbf{u}}f(x,y)=\\frac{(6xy-4y^{3}-4)(1)}{2}+\\frac{(3x^{2}-12xy^{2}+6y)\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\ D_{\\mathbf{u}}f(3,4)=\\frac{72-256-4}{2}+\\frac{(27-576+24)\\sqrt{3}}{2}=-94-\\frac{525\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\end{array}$",
    "hint": "Calculate the partial derivatives and determine the value of $\\theta .$"
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "cp2",
   "source_id": "fs-id1167793241201",
   "source_number": "Checkpoint 14.29",
   "kind": "checkpoint",
   "bloom": "Apply",
   "place": {
    "at": "inline",
    "after": "fs-id1167793990054"
   },
   "cite": "fs-id1167794122930",
   "prompt": "Find the gradient $\\nabla f(x,y)$ of $f(x,y)=(x^{2}-3y^{2})/(2x+y).$",
   "answer": {
    "type": "open",
    "generated_by": "source",
    "solution": "$\\nabla f(x,y)=\\frac{2x^{2}+2xy+6y^{2}}{{(2x+y)}^{2}}\\mathbf{i}-\\frac{x^{2}+12xy+3y^{2}}{{(2x+y)}^{2}}\\mathbf{j}$",
    "hint": "Calculate the partial derivatives, then use <a href=\"#fs-id1167793931842\">Equation 14.39</a>."
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "cp3",
   "source_id": "fs-id1167794293307",
   "source_number": "Checkpoint 14.30",
   "kind": "checkpoint",
   "bloom": "Apply",
   "place": {
    "at": "inline",
    "after": "fs-id1167793931193"
   },
   "cite": "fs-id1167794067004",
   "prompt": "Find the direction for which the directional derivative of $g(x,y)=4x-xy+2y^{2}$ at $(-2,3)$ is a maximum. What is the maximum value?",
   "answer": {
    "type": "open",
    "generated_by": "source",
    "solution": "The gradient of $g$ at $(-2,3)$ is $\\nabla g(-2,3)=\\mathbf{i}+14\\mathbf{j}.$ The unit vector that points in the same direction as $\\nabla g(-2,3)$ is $\\frac{\\nabla g(-2,3)}{\\| \\nabla g(-2,3)\\|}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{197}}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{14}{\\sqrt{197}}\\mathbf{j}=\\frac{\\sqrt{197}}{197}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{14\\sqrt{197}}{197}\\mathbf{j},$ which gives an angle of $\\theta =\\operatorname{arcsin}((14\\sqrt{197})/197)\\approx 1.499\\;\\text{rad}.$ The maximum value of the directional derivative is $\\| \\nabla g(-2,3)\\| =\\sqrt{197}.$",
    "hint": "Evaluate the gradient of $g$ at point $(-2,3).$"
   }
  },
